# SYNOPSIS

```text
echo start gpu-test >/svc/launcher/ctl
```

# DESCRIPTION

gpu-test is an app of the board images: it runs only when the launcher is asked to start it (launcher(8)), and prints its lines to the log.
Before it looks for a GPU it compiles three SPIR-V shaders with the compiler built into it (below), so the compile is checked and timed where no GPU is present too.
It is a client of the GPU class's `csf` capset (gpu-csf-context(5)): the first GPU under `/dev` (`/dev/gpu<n>` or `/dev/display<n>`, its `gpu` service) that offers the capset.
Where none does, as in QEMU, it first says why (what `/dev` holds, and for each `display` or `gpu` entry whether its `gpu` node answers and which capsets it offers), then prints `gpu-test: skipped: no csf GPU` and exits 0.

It makes a context with a VM, a buffer of two pages bound at an address of its own, a group of one queue and a sync, and runs four things on it.
First, a stream it wrote itself: MOV48 and STORE_STATE of the GPU's timestamp into the buffer, then MOV48 and SYNC_ADD64 of 1 into a counter word of the buffer.
It submits it with its sync as the signal and waits up to 10 seconds for the counter to reach 1, then reads the buffer back: the timestamp is not 0, the counter word is 1, and the group's state is clean.
The check prints `gpu-test: ok: the GPU ran a command stream` and the timestamp.
Second, a stream whose CALL goes to an address nothing maps.
The GPU's MMU faults, the group ends, and the sync is raised all the same.
The group's state has the fatal fault bit and names its queue, and a further submit on the context fails with `lost`.
Then a fresh context makes the first stream again, and it runs.
The check prints `gpu-test: ok: a faulting stream ended only its group`.
Third, a hundred jobs with no stream, each submitted with a signal and waited for: the median of the time from the submit to the counter's rise, as `budget name=gpu.mali.empty_job_us value=<us> unit=us`.

Fourth, in a context of its own with a second buffer for the output, a compute shader.
It first reads the context's thread limits (THREAD_MAX_THREADS, THREAD_MAX_WORKGROUP_SIZE and THREAD_FEATURES, from the driver's `limits` call) and fails if any is missing or zero; it prints `gpu-test: ok: thread limits:` with the threads a core, the workgroup limit, the registers and tasks a core and the core count, and sizes the dispatch from them: the workgroups of a task and the per-core limit of the run (the tasks a core runs, on the 12th architecture and later).
The program lays a dispatch out in a block of GPU memory: the shader program descriptor, the local storage descriptor, one word of FAU holding the address of an output buffer, a shader of two instructions written by hand, and the command stream that sets up the queue, requests the compute resource, loads the run state, runs the shader over 4 workgroups of 16 invocations, waits for every scoreboard slot and cleans the L2 and LSC caches for the CPU.
Each invocation stores its global invocation id at its own index of the output buffer.
The program reads back 64 words and checks every one: word i reads i, and the word after them is untouched.
The check prints `gpu-test: ok: a compute shader ran`.
Then it runs 20 dispatches of one workgroup of 64 invocations, each submitted with a signal and waited for: the median of the time from the submit to the counter's rise, as `budget name=gpu.mali.compute_dispatch_us value=<us> unit=us`.

Fifth, a triangle.
In a context of its own with buffers for the render pass's records (descriptors, shaders and command streams), a 64 by 64 linear RGBA8 colour target filled with a marker value, the tiler heap's context and its one 1 MiB chunk, and the 64 KiB geometry buffer, it draws one triangle with two shaders written by hand from the instruction set's tables.
The vertex shader reads each vertex's window-space position from a table in memory by the vertex id and stores it in the hardware's vertex buffer; the fragment shader writes one constant colour, orange (red 1.0, green 0.5, alpha 1.0), through an opaque blend descriptor.
The pass is the tiler context and heap descriptor, the framebuffer descriptor with one render target cleared to opaque black, and a stream that requests the tiler, IDVS and fragment resources, sets the heap, runs the draw, finishes the tiling, waits for it, runs the fragment pass over the whole frame, hands the heap's chunks back and cleans the caches for the CPU.
The program tries two layouts of that stream, each in a context of its own, and reports the first that draws: two queues, a vertex/tiler queue that signals a sync object in memory and a fragment queue that waits for it, then one queue that does both.
It reads the target back and compares every pixel with a CPU rasterisation of the same triangle at pixel centres.
A pixel within one pixel of an edge is not judged, because the fill rule is not known; every other pixel must be the triangle's colour (green 127 or 128) or exactly the clear, and at least 200 must be inside.
The check prints `gpu-test: ok: a triangle was drawn` with how many pixels were inside, outside and not judged and the layout's time, then `budget name=gpu.mali.triangle_ms value=<ms> unit=ms`: the median over up to 8 further passes of the time from the submit to the counter's rise (the first pass's, if they do not finish), rounded up to a whole millisecond.
A layout that does not draw prints why (the group's state, or how many pixels were wrong with the first one, saying where the buffer was never written, was only cleared, or matches the image turned upside down); if neither draws the check fails.
Sixth, three shaders compiled on the device.
The SPIR-V is made on the host and committed as arrays of words (`generated/CompilerShaders.swift`, the corpus's bring-up, saxpy and reduction shaders);
the Valhall backend (valhallbackend(2)) compiles each when the program starts, before any GPU is looked for.
For each it prints `gpu-test: compiled <name>:` with the instructions, registers, spills, workgroup memory and the compile's time in microseconds, and, after the three, the slowest compile in whole milliseconds, rounded up, as `budget name=compiler.valhall.compile_ms value=<ms> unit=ms`.
A compile the backend refuses fails the program.
Each shader is then dispatched in a context of its own.
The FAU block is filled from the layout the backend returns (`FauLayout.block`: the buffers' addresses in (set, binding) order, then the push constants), the thread storage and the workgroup memory are allocated at the sizes the thread limits give, and the same dispatch recipe runs it.
Every buffer is followed by four words of a poison value, and the check fails if one changes.
The bring-up shader stores each invocation's global id into 64 words (4 workgroups of 16): word i reads i, and the check prints `gpu-test: ok: compiled globalID ran`.
Saxpy computes y = a * x + y for the first 100 of 128 elements, with a and 100 as push constants, and the check compares the floats bit for bit with the CPU's multiply followed by an add, each rounded (the backend emits a multiply and an add, not a fused one); it prints `gpu-test: ok: compiled saxpy ran`.
The reduction sums each workgroup of 64 inputs in workgroup memory with barriers and writes one word per workgroup; the four sums are compared exactly, and the check prints `gpu-test: ok: compiled reduce ran`.
A compiled check that fails prints `gpu-test: FAILED:` and the run goes on to the rest; the program exits 1 after the compiled shaders.
After saxpy's check, pass or fail, it runs probes: variants of saxpy's listing, assembled and validated on the host (the ISA's assembler reads Characters, which this program can't link), dispatched over the same buffers and FAU block, each printing `gpu-test: probe <name>: word0 <hex> word1 <hex> (expected <hex> <hex>)`.
The variants store x unchanged, a (a FADD of the FAU word and zero, and an IADD_IMM), a times x (the FMA as the compiler writes it with a negative-zero addend, without the last-use mark, with a register zero or a plain zero addend, with a in a register, and as FMUL), x plus y, the whole listing, the listing with its wait covering both loads, and the listing with its FMA writing another register.
Four more show where a wait takes effect: a wait before the read and one on a NOP that names the wrong slot, the read carrying the wait itself, and a load carrying the wait on its own slot.
A wait in an instruction's flow field takes effect after that instruction, so the read that carries the wait itself, and the compiler's earlier saxpy (every wait on its reader), are expected to store garbage: the probes print them as `expected failure`.
Before them it prints the compiler's code words beside the listing's, each with the listing's disassembled line, marking each word that differs.

Each check that fails prints `gpu-test: FAILED:` and what failed, and the program exits 1.
Its last line is `gpu-test: passed`.

