# BUGS

The empty job's time is the driver's round trip, not the GPU's: a job with no stream is done when the jobs before it are, and with none before it, at once.
The first stream is only the instructions a kernel driver knows.
The hand-assembled compute shader is two instructions written from the instruction set's tables; it exercises the run state, the descriptors and the memory path, and no arithmetic beyond one 64-bit add.
The compiled shaders are three, none using the texture, varying or tile instructions, and the compile's time is of this program's own run on the board's CPU.
The compiler's strings are compared as bytes (the device's Embedded Swift links no Unicode tables), so a SPIR-V entry-point name outside ASCII is compared by its bytes.
The triangle's tiler heap is the program's own memory, not a kernel heap: the csf capset has no call to create one, so the heap context is 64 zero bytes of a buffer and a tiler out-of-memory event cannot be answered; one 1 MiB chunk is far above what a triangle needs.
The vertex shader runs as one program for the position and varying passes and has no varyings; the draw's varying size is zero.
The primitive flags' view mask is zero, which the spec gives only for layered and multiview passes.
The fill rule is unknown, so the edge of the triangle is not checked.
The repeat passes rewrite the pass's records, which resets the heap descriptor, over a heap whose context the firmware has already used.
The fault check relies on the MMU fault ending the group: a GPU that reports it otherwise fails the check.
The stream sets up the queue's scoreboard state and requests the compute resource in its first run, and the timed runs rely on both persisting in the group.
The fault check relies on the MMU fault ending the group: a GPU that reports it otherwise fails the check.
